WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.820
What do you think of when
you think of a battery?

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.920
For most, it's what we
use to power devices

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.133
for both work and play
and maybe even your car.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.770
Over the past few decades,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.020
they've gotten way more powerful,

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.033
long lasting and affordable.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.830
But all of this is just a prologue

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.740
to what the next
batteries are going to do.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.180
As we drive to increasingly
renewable power driven grid,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.960
we also need to be able to store
energy and release it later

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.660
to cover those periods of
weather intermittencies

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.910
that are driving this
new grid that we're in.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.620
It's a multi-trillion dollar opportunity

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.220
and it's imperative that we
figure out the solution here.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.890
How we store energy on a massive scale

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.410
is in many ways the central challenge

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.393
of the fight to stop climate change.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.030
And the solutions we're coming up with

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.430
might not be what you think.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.120
The need for massive batteries

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.250
stems from an aspect of electricity

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.230
that we don't often think about.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.270
So when we make electricity,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.030
we produce it and we use
it almost instantaneously

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.963
because of this inability to store.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.790
When we turn on a button
or switch on a light,

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:37.380
at that very moment somebody
somewhere has generated

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.203
electricity for you to be able to do that.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.980
That exact electricity
that's making that light

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.760
just had to be produced
somewhere within about a minute.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.900
It's very, very new,
the power that we use.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.630
If there's not enough
electricity being produced,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.160
we get what we all know

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.010
actually, which is called a brownout.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.610
Which means there's not quite
enough electricity coming in

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.693
to power stuff.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.290
And if you have too much electricity,

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.273
that will also bring down the system.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.010
So you can't produce a
lot more than you're using

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.980
and you can't produce a
lot less than you're using.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.750
For most of the grid's history,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.760
this hasn't been much of a problem.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.430
We've got our power from
steady, reliable sources

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.013
like coal plants and hydroelectric dams.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.420
But now of course, that's all changing.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.980
We can adjust the coal fire power plant,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.990
how much electricity it's making,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.023
but we can't adjust how much
wind a wind turbine is making.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.810
Wind and solar power

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.170
can't always give us the
juice right when we need it.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:49.020
But if we could save up
energy from renewables

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.790
and release it when it's needed,

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.940
clean energy could be as reliable as coal.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.090
The amount of energy storage
we need is going to grow

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.370
because we are going to have
to rely on solar and wind

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.550
which are more intermittent,
but it's also going to grow

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.710
because the pure amount of electricity

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.713
that we are going to use going
forward is going to grow.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:14.280
So dozens of companies are
working on gigantic batteries

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.883
hoping to store enough energy
to kick our fossil fuel habit.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.853
And one of the biggest
batteries is this mountain.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.380
FirstLight Power, we are today,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.920
the largest portfolio of
operating renewable energy

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.683
and energy storage in
the New England region.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.237
We are using a mountain as a giant battery

00:03:45.237 --> 00:03:46.953
and that's what we do here.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.760
Being here, inside Northfield Mountain,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.313
it's an incredibly unique facility.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.330
We are carved out of the
inside of a mountain,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.970
it is a facility that dates back 50 years

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.600
and yet at the same
time is ideally situated

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.483
to drive the energy
transition of the future.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.583
So pump storage is the oldest
form of energy storage.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.470
It's essentially transferring water

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.660
from an upper reservoir
to a lower reservoir

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.560
and back and forth throughout the day.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:24.540
Our lower reservoir is
the Connecticut River

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.690
which is flowing by about a
mile from where we're standing.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.160
And then our upper reservoir
is a man-made dam, essentially,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.680
on top of a mountain.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.800
Water is pumped up to the upper reservoir

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.680
and stored for whenever it's needed later.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.170
And then it flows down
through turbine generators

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.613
to generate electricity.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.570
If that sounds like a
giant battery charging

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.303
and discharging, well, exactly.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.320
So what we're looking at here

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.320
is one of the four units
at Northfield in pump mode.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.020
So right now we are pumping
water from the Connecticut River

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.343
to the upper reservoir.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.250
Later on, we'll use that same water

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.840
spin the machine the other direction

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.090
and generate electricity.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.410
When we're standing at
Northfield Mountain,

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:21.030
we're talking about 1,200
megawatts of instantaneous power.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.860
We can provide enough power

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.980
to support roughly a
million New England homes

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.840
on any given day.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.490
Pumped hydro storage is more
than a century year old.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:35.160
It was initially used to be
able to just generate hydropower

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.070
when you wanted it and then in the '70s

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.590
when you had the creation of nuclear power

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.470
where power plants had
to be run all the time

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.170
even when there wasn't
demand for electricity,

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.986
pumped hydro storage became

00:05:47.986 --> 00:05:50.370
the stores of excess electricity.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.910
Atomic energy, the reality
for homes and factories

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.923
and schools all over the world.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.540
But today with the nuclear
industry in decline,

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.020
the mountain has had to
find a new niche to fill.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.290
So rather than pairing with nuclear power,

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.470
we're a great pair to solar or wind

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.750
or other intermittent renewables.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.750
Offshore wind has been a long
time coming in New England.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.510
It's been on the drawing
books for a number of years,

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.810
but we are now seeing the
first very large projects

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.280
come to fruition.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.190
So the opportunity for a
facility like Northfield Mountain

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:31.190
is to provide that balance
to large scale offshore wind

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.713
and store it for times when
that electricity is needed.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.170
Unfortunately, mountain-sized batteries

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.633
do have some unique limitations.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.420
The trouble is that pumped hydro storage

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.493
requires a specific kind of geography.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.900
Typically, hills with either a river

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.910
or lots of access to water

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:59.640
in a form of rainfall that is consistent

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.160
to be able to make it
an economical project

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.370
that you can build and then
operate for decades to come.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.290
And that's not always feasible

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:10.980
because of the lack of
mountains or lack of water.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.890
Some of the obstacles
facing large scale build out

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:17.850
of new pumped storage
projects; one is cost.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.490
These projects are billion
dollar projects now.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.850
They require ongoing
significant capital investment

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.123
to make sure that they can
continue to run reliably.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.000
And eventually we will run out

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.460
of how much pumped hydro storage can do.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:36.600
So we are going to have to
need other solutions as well

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:37.893
to fill those gaps.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.730
Form Energy is developing
the kind of energy storage

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.520
you need to enable the
complete decarbonization

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.693
of the electric system.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.350
It's a battery that's dramatically cheaper

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.660
than anything else that's out there today

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.190
and is also made of materials

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.863
that scale to the size of the challenge.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.540
Co-founded by former
Tesla VP, Mateo Jaramillo,

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.940
Form Energy is making
a new kind of battery.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.420
They hope can store
energy on a massive scale.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.820
An iron-air battery.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.050
When we talk about batteries

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.060
we kind of think of these black boxes,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.370
but really what goes inside that black box

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.220
can be very different
chemistries and different metals

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.240
that enable those batteries
to do different things.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.210
Take the example of lithium-ion batteries.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.663
These are what go inside electric cars.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.200
In a car, you want it to go fast

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.430
so you want it to draw
electricity at very high rates

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:41.613
from the battery into the car
and then drive it forward.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.580
Lithium-ion batteries are super powerful

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.190
but relatively expensive.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.430
Batteries that store massive
amounts of energy on the grid

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.590
are going to have to be way cheaper

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.780
in order to build them at scale.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.600
To be able to build a
battery that is really cheap,

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.610
one of the things that
you're going to require

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.523
is using materials that are very cheap.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.340
Iron is really, really cheap,

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:08.040
and it's really really
abundant in the earth's crust.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.610
And if anybody's familiar
with iron it's that it rusts.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.883
So we are rusting and unrusting
iron. That's the battery.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.450
When iron takes on oxygen,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.400
that means it's giving off an electron.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.910
That process which is
really a chemical reaction,

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.770
a nuisance for most of us

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.440
is also a process that generates energy

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.810
which could be converted into electricity.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.390
And then when you want
to store electricity

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:37.320
into that battery, you convert
that rust back into iron.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.323
And that's really how
simple that battery is.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:43.080
It's never been commercialized before

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.903
but it has been understood
for about 50 years.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.823
So this is the iron material,

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:56.733
which is in these pellets.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:05.490
There will be many, many
kilograms in each repeat unit

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:06.323
of the cell.

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:13.670
So I set this cell to charge,
I'm putting energy into it.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:18.860
And when we do that charge
process, we unrust the iron.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.200
The big things that you
can see on the outside

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.230
of this battery are iron electrodes.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:27.840
And then we generate oxygen.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.960
So the oxygen comes off in
tiny, tiny little bubbles

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.243
and they flow around on the inside.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.770
We're standing in front of an incubator

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.370
full of subscale cells
that we are testing.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.240
So these are miniature
versions of the big cell

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:48.510
that we use to test out
different material combinations,

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:49.590
different designs,

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.860
different conditions that we
cycle the batteries under.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.623
And we have 2,000 of
these all over this lab.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:01.260
It is still the scrappy
problem solving atmosphere

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.690
of a startup even though
we're getting bigger

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:07.770
and constantly problem
solving on your feet

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:10.203
trying things that have
never been tried before.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.610
Form Energy has been going strong

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:16.020
in the last couple of years,

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:19.560
raising more than $350 million to date.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.950
But an entirely new
battery chemistry like this

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.353
still has a long road
ahead to prove itself.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:29.880
It took about 30 years

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.370
from when the first lithium-ion battery

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.530
was put in a camcorder

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:38.160
to it becoming a mainstream
battery that powers

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.933
all electric cars in the world.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.470
Iron-air batteries are
going to have to do that

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:46.020
but in a much more compressed period.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.170
Form Energy has only been
around for about five years

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:52.710
and it's going to have to show
its commercial applications

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.300
within the next five years.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.810
That's shrinking the
development time down to a third

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:00.000
and that's no easy challenge.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.000
So as we're commercializing
this iron-air chemistry

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.110
for the first time,

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:08.130
the challenge is to demonstrate
unequivocally with data

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:11.640
that it is a reliable durable
piece of infrastructure

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.030
that scales to the existing
infrastructure that's out there.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:19.170
So we are already building at
the intended production scale.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:21.630
So this is a meter cubed
device that we have

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:24.213
and we're already producing
those devices today.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:33.963
The idea of the energy
transition can seem daunting.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.350
The current energy system just works

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.783
aside from the whole
melting the planet thing.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:48.030
But the gigantic battery
industry is growing fast

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.910
and other solutions are
gaining steam as well

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:53.280
like storing energy with compressed air

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.653
or using hydrogen as a clean fuel.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.540
A total carbon free grid is
getting easier and easier

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:01.770
to imagine.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.950
It's just a question of whether
we can get there in time.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.410
We are going to, I think as a society,

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.980
really have to embrace our
ability to do big things,

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.500
to build a large energy infrastructure

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.000
if we're going to succeed

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.850
in what is the defining
challenge of our time

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:22.140
and that is building a clean
energy system for the future.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.960
It's quite easy for us to
know what success looks like

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.380
for Form Energy, and that is
having the impact at scale

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:31.290
on the decarbonization
effort for the electric grid.

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:34.740
And that is measured at
no less than gigatons.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.440
So billions of tons of carbon

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:40.380
that do not have to be
released into the atmosphere

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:41.850
any longer.

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:44.340
It's really nice to be
working on something

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:46.560
that I think is actually
going to make a difference

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:47.670
in the world.

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:49.970
Makes me a little more
motivated to do my work

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.830
to feel like it's actually
going towards something

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:53.780
I care about a lot.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:58.260
And as an engineer, that
the problems I'm solving

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.943
are problems that matter.

